    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tMay 5, 1970, Alex Pappas, New York, sold to André Emmerich, New York (Z-92) [1]; September 1970, sold to Munson-Williams-Proctor Arts Institute, Utica, New York (70.83.1) [2]; about 2000, sold to/through Ancient Art of the New World, New York [3]; May 22, 2000, sold to US private collection [4]; 2016, sold to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] According to the Emmerich inventory card and dated slide in the curatorial file.\n\t<br />\n\t[2] According to an invoice in the curatorial file.\n\t<br />\n\t[3] Ancient Art of the New World, New York, was an agent for Munson-Williams-Proctor Arts Institute\n\t<br />\n\t[4] According to US private collection.\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"This Beni Zaa offering vessel is among the finest examples known. The rear of the object consists of a simple cylindrical vessel, while the front is composed of numerous mold-impressed clay sections collectively forming a male figure, likely an ancestor. Such vessels have been found in architecturally elaborate tombs located under residential compounds at Monte Albán, Oaxaca, the primary center of Beni Zaa culture for thousands of years. Most funerary vessels have been found empty, and it is unclear whether they ever held substances: The vessel may instead have been conceived as a receptacle for the soul of the ancestor depicted on it.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA3_20-T6-3_CLA_FA.pdf  - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
